**Obesity** means carrying extra body fat that can affect your health. It is very common. Around **1 in 4 adults in England** live with obesity.

Obesity is recognised as a **long-term health condition**. Like other conditions, it has many causes, and it can be treated and managed with the right support.

## How obesity is measured

Doctors usually use your **body mass index (BMI)**, which compares your weight with your height. You can work yours out with the NHS BMI calculator.

| BMI | What it usually means |
| --- | --- |
| 18.5 to 24.9 | Healthy weight |
| 25 to 29.9 | Overweight |
| 30 to 39.9 | Obesity |
| 40 or more | Severe obesity |

If you are of **South Asian, Chinese, other Asian, Middle Eastern, Black African or African-Caribbean** heritage, health risks start at a lower BMI. For you, **23** is the start of overweight and **27.5** is the start of obesity.

Your **waist** also matters. As a guide, try to keep your waist to **less than half your height**.

## Why it matters for health

Obesity raises the risk of many conditions, including:

- **type 2 diabetes**
- **high blood pressure** and **heart disease**
- **osteoarthritis**, especially in the knees and hips
- **some cancers**
- **sleep apnoea** and breathing problems
- **low mood** and anxiety

Losing even a small amount of weight, such as **5% of your body weight**, can improve your health and lower these risks.

## What causes obesity?

Many things play a part, often together:

- **genes**, which affect appetite and how your body stores fat
- **the food around us**, which is often cheap, high in calories and heavily advertised
- **less active lives**, with more sitting at work and at home
- **some medicines**, such as some antidepressants, steroids and diabetes medicines
- **health conditions**, such as an underactive thyroid
- **sleep, stress and mental health**
- **money and time**, which affect what food you can buy and cook

## Getting support

You do not have to do it alone. Your GP can check your health, and may refer you to an **NHS weight management programme** or, for some people, specialist treatment. See [obesity treatment and support](/conditions/physical/obesity/treatment/).

{% callout type="good" title="Talking to your GP" %}
It is fine to ask your GP for help with your weight. You can say, "I would like some support with my weight. What is available to me?"
{% /callout %}
